<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nasrullah Khan (emir of Bukhara)</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/1860">1860</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nasrullah Khan was emir of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bukhara">Bukhara</a> from 1827 to 1860, of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/manghit-dynasty">Manghit dynasty</a>. He seized the throne after his father's death, eliminating his brothers, and spent his reign centralising power at the expense of the tribal nobility and fighting the Khanate of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kokand">Kokand</a>. He became notorious in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/britain">Britain</a> for executing the British officers <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-stoddart">Charles Stoddart</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arthur-conolly">Arthur Conolly</a> in 1842.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nasrullah-khan-emir-of-bukhara.jpg" alt="Nasrullah Khan (emir of Bukhara)" width="320" height="418" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Khanikov, Belousov · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Nasrulla.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
